::MCR_U03_L08_GP_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L08_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: Unit 3 standards synthesis
Question:
Which display best compares category counts?
{
=A. bar graph#Correct. A bar graph compares frequencies across categories.
~B. histogram#A histogram groups numerical data into intervals.
~C. scatterplot#A scatterplot shows paired numerical data.
~D. line of fit#A line of fit models bivariate numerical data.
}
::MCR_U03_L08_GP_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L08_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: Unit 3 standards synthesis
Question:
A data set has an extreme high value. Which center is usually more resistant?
{
~A. mean#Mean uses every value and can be pulled upward.
=B. median#Correct. Median depends on position and is less affected by an extreme value.
~C. range#Range measures spread, not center.
~D. maximum#Maximum is the extreme value itself.
}
::MCR_U03_L08_GP_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L08_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: Unit 3 standards synthesis
Question:
A scatterplot rises from left to right. What association is shown?
{
~A. negative association#A negative association falls from left to right.
~B. no association#A clear upward pattern is an association.
=C. positive association#Correct. As x increases, y tends to increase.
~D. sampling bias#This describes sample selection, not scatterplot direction.
}
::MCR_U03_L08_GP_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L08_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: Unit 3 standards synthesis
Question:
A survey uses only volunteers from an online post. What concern should be stated?
{
~A. the data prove causation#A volunteer survey does not prove cause and effect.
~B. the sample is automatically representative#Volunteer samples can be biased.
~C. the graph must be a histogram#The issue is sample design, not display type.
=D. voluntary response bias may limit the conclusion#Correct. Volunteers may not represent the full population.
}
::MCR_U03_L08_GP_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L08_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: Unit 3 standards synthesis
Question:
Which conclusion is most responsible?
{
~A. This proves the program works for everyone.#This overgeneralizes beyond the sample.
=B. The sample suggests a pattern, but more representative data are needed.#Correct. This states the pattern and the limit.
~C. The data are useless.#Limited data can still provide cautious evidence.
~D. The highest value is always the answer.#One value is not enough for a full conclusion.
}
